# Practical No. 4(A)

## Aim
To understand and demonstrate React component lifecycle methods using a class component.

## Code

### `src/App.js`

```jsx
import React from "react";
import LifecycleClass from "./components/LifecycleClass";

function App() {
  return <LifecycleClass />;
}

export default App;
```

### `src/components/LifecycleClass.jsx`

```jsx
import React, { Component } from "react";

class LifecycleClass extends Component {
  state = { count: 0 };

  componentDidMount() {
    console.log("componentDidMount: Component mounted");
  }

  componentDidUpdate() {
    console.log("componentDidUpdate: Component updated");
  }

  componentWillUnmount() {
    console.log("componentWillUnmount: Component removed");
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h2>Count: {this.state.count}</h2>
        <button
          onClick={() =>
            this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 })
          }
        >
          Update Count
        </button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

export default LifecycleClass;
```

## Output

Initially:

```text
Count: 0
[Update Count]
```

After clicking **Update Count**:

```text
Count: 1
[Update Count]
```

Console output:

```text
componentDidMount: Component mounted
componentDidUpdate: Component updated
```

## Result
Successfully demonstrated the component lifecycle methods `componentDidMount()`, `componentDidUpdate()`, and `componentWillUnmount()` using a React class component.
